title: VAPOR PANDA / ARTEX
page: breaches/vapor-panda-artex.html
as-of: 2026-11-20
promise: AI agents, steered by a human at decision points, targeted 200+ organizations in 11 days, per CrowdStrike.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 200+ | organizations targeted | in at least 14 countries over 11 days, per CrowdStrike
stat: 9 | organizations with confirmed post-exploitation | per CrowdStrike Intelligence
stat: 1,500+ | commands an hour possible with ARTEX | per CrowdStrike, against up to 40 for hands-on work
CrowdStrike attributes the campaign to VAPOR PANDA, a China-nexus adversary. It says password spraying and credential scanning hit at least 12 more organizations.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

CrowdStrike reports that VAPOR PANDA, a China-nexus adversary, used the ARTEX agentic framework against more than 200 organizations in at least 14 countries over 11 days. Targets included foreign ministries, US semiconductor, defense and think tank organizations, and telecommunications providers.

CrowdStrike Intelligence confirmed post-exploitation at nine of them, and says password spraying and credential scanning hit at least 12 more.

The third figure is about speed. Adam Meyers of CrowdStrike puts typical hands-on activity at up to about 40 commands an hour, and said ARTEX could exceed 1,500.

Keep the attributions when you say these numbers aloud. Each one is a vendor's claim, and the page reports it as one. CrowdStrike also says VAPOR PANDA has not previously been seen using AI-driven intrusion tooling.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: October 17, 2026 | CrowdStrike OverWatch sees the campaign begin
event: Over the next 11 days | Agents target 200+ organizations in at least 14 countries, per CrowdStrike
event: October 30 | CyberScoop reports the campaign as active
event: Early November | Censys counts 1,465 hosts exposing an ARTEX console, of any operator
VAPOR PANDA, a China-nexus adversary per CrowdStrike, used ARTEX against targets including foreign ministries, US semiconductor, defense and think tank organizations, and telecommunications providers. The page gives no end date. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives only a few dates, and the timeline shows only those.

CrowdStrike OverWatch saw the campaign begin on October 17, 2026. Over the next 11 days, CrowdStrike reports, VAPOR PANDA used ARTEX against more than 200 organizations. The page gives no end date, so do not count forward from October 17 to name one.

CyberScoop reported the campaign as active on October 30. In early November, Censys counted hosts with an exposed ARTEX console. That count covers exposed ARTEX consoles whoever runs them, and the page does not tie those hosts to VAPOR PANDA. It comes back in a few slides, beside other counts that disagree with it.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps between them are not to scale. If someone asks whether the campaign is over, the answer from the page is that it does not say.

== Nine confirmed, about 140 reported
layout: compare
col: 9 confirmed | From CrowdStrike's published report | CrowdStrike Intelligence confirmed post-exploitation at these organizations | The figure the csoh.org kill chain uses for confirmed intrusions
col: About 140 reported | From CrowdStrike's comments to CyberScoop | About 70% of targets, confirmed or with moderate-confidence evidence of intrusion | Mixes confirmed cases with moderate-confidence ones
CrowdStrike's report and the interview measure different things, so this deck uses nine for confirmed intrusions.
notes:
Slow down on this slide. Two figures circulate for how many organizations were compromised, and they measure different things.

Nine is the number of organizations where CrowdStrike Intelligence confirmed post-exploitation, in its published report.

About 140 comes from CrowdStrike's comments to CyberScoop, where about 70% of targets were either confirmed or showed moderate-confidence evidence of intrusion. That larger figure mixes confirmed cases with moderate-confidence ones, so it cannot stand in for a count of confirmed compromises.

The csoh.org kill chain uses nine for confirmed intrusions, and this deck does the same. When you repeat the 140, say where it came from and that it includes moderate-confidence cases.

== How VAPOR PANDA ran ARTEX, per CrowdStrike
layout: layers
layer: Human | An operator, stepping in to steer at decision points
layer: ARTEX agents | Main agent | Planner agents | Worker agents
layer: Shared state | A graph of facts, findings and goals
CrowdStrike says the operation ran primarily on the DeepSeek model, with a second commercial model it could not confirm. One intrusion's planning agents consumed on the order of 1.4 billion input tokens.
notes:
ARTEX is an agentic framework. It coordinates a main agent with planner agents and worker agents over a shared graph of facts, findings and goals.

This slide shows how VAPOR PANDA ran it, as CrowdStrike reports: a human operator at the top, then the agents, then the shared graph they work over. The human tier describes this campaign. Check Point found that most operators of the 10 cybercrime instances it examined barely interacted with their agents once the initial goal was set, as the next slide shows. The three kinds of agent sit side by side because the page names them without saying how work passes between them, and the arrows show the layering only.

CrowdStrike says the operation ran primarily on the DeepSeek model, with a second, subscription-based commercial model it could not confirm. One intrusion's planning agents consumed on the order of 1.4 billion input tokens, which the page treats as a marker of scale.

The operator sits at the top because recovered transcripts show Chinese-language instructions labeled as human input.

== How much humans steer depends on the operator
layout: compare
col: VAPOR PANDA, per CrowdStrike | Chinese-language instructions labeled as human input directed the agents | The operator intervened only to steer at decision points | The agents' breadth likely lets a single operator reach more targets at once
col: 10 cybercrime instances, per Check Point | Operators pursued financially motivated cybercrime, Check Point says | Most barely interacted with their agents once the initial goal was set | They mostly asked for progress or pointed agents at a new target
Human involvement varies between operators.
notes:
How much a human steers ARTEX depends on who is running it.

In VAPOR PANDA's case, recovered transcripts show Chinese-language instructions labeled as human input, directing agents to write web shells, extract domain administrator passwords and inventory endpoint sensor coverage. CrowdStrike says the operator intervened only to steer at decision points, and that this breadth likely enables a single operator to apply more techniques against more targets at once. Keep the word likely. The page does not say how many operators ran the campaign.

Check Point looked at something different: the full databases of 10 misconfigured ARTEX instances whose operators, it says, pursued financially motivated cybercrime. Most of those actors barely interacted with their agents once the initial goal was set. Models of the DeepSeek Flash family were the most common in those instances. Of the three instances configured with American models, Check Point saw guardrails hit only with Anthropic's Opus 5.5, and that actor then switched to more permissive Chinese models.

== Exposed ARTEX consoles, by counting method
layout: table
row: Source | Method | What it found
row: Censys | Page title plus Next.js assets | 1,465 hosts, early November
row: Censys | Two hosting blocks set aside | About 815 distinct-looking deployments
row: Check Point | Its own metadata analysis | 1,608 live, publicly reachable instances
row: Check Point | The same analysis | 2,557 that host or have hosted a panel
row: Check Point | Summary atop the same report | 1,800+ publicly exposed instances
To reach about 815, Censys set aside two blocks of identically configured hosting. It cautions that not every instance is operated with malicious intent, and that some exposed hosts are likely run by authorized testers.
notes:
Counting exposed ARTEX consoles depends on the method, and the page sets the counts side by side without choosing between them.

Censys matched the console's page title together with its Next.js assets and counted 1,465 hosts with an exposed console in early November. Once two blocks of identically configured hosting are set aside, it estimates about 815 distinct-looking deployments.

Check Point, using its own metadata analysis, counted 1,608 live, publicly reachable instances and 2,557 systems that host or have hosted an ARTEX panel. The summary at the top of the same report says more than 1,800 publicly exposed instances.

Keep Censys's caution with these numbers. Not every instance is operated with malicious intent, and some exposed hosts are likely run by authorized testers.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Resource Development | Human-steered agents | T1588.007
step: 02 | Initial Access | Public apps and valid accounts | T1190
step: 03 | Credential Access | Keys nobody rotated | T1606
step: 04 | Credential Access | The metadata service | T1552.005
step: 05 | Execution and Privilege Escalation | Containers, AD | T1609
step: 06 | Discovery | Hosts nobody watches | T1518.001
step: 07 | Stealth and Collection | BLOODALCHEMY | T1574.001
Stages draw on intrusions at several organizations; arrows show phase order only.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is how the operation was resourced, with agents doing the work and a human at the decision points. Stage 02 is initial access through public-facing applications and valid accounts. Stages 03 and 04 are credential access, first through keys nobody rotated and then through the cloud metadata service. Stage 05, under Execution and Privilege Escalation, covers containers and Active Directory. Stage 06, under Discovery, covers finding the hosts no sensor watches. Stage 07, under Stealth and Collection, covers the BLOODALCHEMY backdoor and the mail.

The stages draw on intrusions at several organizations, among them a Greek government entity, a US pharmaceutical organization and two ministries. Read the arrows as the order of phases across the campaign. The page does not trace one victim through all seven stages. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== A human steered agents at decision points
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1588.007 | Obtain Capabilities: Artificial Intelligence
fact: Structure | main agent, planner and worker agents, shared graph
fact: Models | primarily DeepSeek, plus a second unconfirmed commercial model
fact: Planning agents | about 1.4 billion input tokens in one intrusion
Recovered transcripts show Chinese-language instructions labeled as human input, directing agents to write web shells, extract domain administrator passwords and inventory endpoint sensor coverage.
notes:
Stage 01 covers the agent setup behind the operation. The page maps it to T1588.007, Obtain Capabilities: Artificial Intelligence.

ARTEX split each intrusion among planner and worker agents, and a human operator stepped in to steer. CrowdStrike says the operation ran primarily on DeepSeek, with a second commercial model it could not confirm. One intrusion's planning agents consumed on the order of 1.4 billion input tokens.

Stress the human role. Recovered transcripts show Chinese-language instructions labeled as human input, directing agents to write web shells, extract domain administrator passwords and inventory endpoint sensor coverage. The page says this was not an unattended system, and CrowdStrike says the operator intervened only to steer at decision points.

The page ties no defense to this stage. That gap comes back in the discussion at the end.

== Agents exploited public applications, once chaining unsigned SAML with XXE
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1190 | Exploit Public-Facing Application
technique: T1110.003 | Brute Force: Password Spraying
fact: Entry | public-facing applications and valid accounts
fact: Paths | web apps, cloud, containers, Active Directory, mail and VPN
fact: Example chain | an unsigned SAML assertion plus an XXE flaw
fact: Elsewhere | password spraying and credential scanning at 12+ organizations
CyberScoop reports requests as often as every 10 seconds, on several paths at once.
notes:
Initial access came through public-facing applications and valid accounts. The page maps it to T1190, Exploit Public-Facing Application, and tags T1110.003 for password spraying.

CrowdStrike describes agents pursuing several attack paths at once across web applications, cloud services, container environments, Active Directory, and mail and VPN infrastructure. When one path failed, they switched to another.

One case chained an unsigned Security Assertion Markup Language assertion with an XML external entity flaw. The next slide explains both ideas in general. CyberScoop reports requests as often as every 10 seconds, with multiple paths running concurrently.

Separately, CrowdStrike says password spraying and credential scanning hit at least 12 more organizations. The page does not say whether any of those were compromised.

== XXE flaws and unsigned SAML assertions
layout: background
source: OWASP XML External Entity Prevention Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_External_Entity_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html
credit: Also from the OWASP SAML Security Cheat Sheet (cheatsheetseries.owasp.org)
An XML parser that resolves external entities in untrusted input can expose local files. That flaw is called XXE.

For SAML sign-in, OWASP says each assertion, or the whole response, must be signed by an authorized identity provider.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains two ideas in general, from OWASP's cheat sheets, and says nothing about the incident.

OWASP describes XML external entity injection as an XML processor resolving an external entity from untrusted input, which can expose local files, cause server-side request forgery, or exhaust resources. Its first advice is to disable document type definitions wherever possible, and otherwise to turn off external entity resolution and external DTD loading.

SAML, the Security Assertion Markup Language, is an open standard for exchanging authentication and authorization information. OWASP names a digital signature with a certified key as the most common way to guarantee message integrity, and lists forged assertions among the attacks it counters. Its guidance for service providers is to make sure each assertion, or the entire response, is signed, and signed by an authorized identity provider. An unsigned assertion leaves the service provider no signature to check.

Also from the OWASP SAML Security Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SAML_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html

== At a Greek government entity, the agent forged an administrator token
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1606 | Forge Web Credentials
technique: T1133 | External Remote Services
fact: From the operator | a 2022 directory export, keys likely not rotated since
fact: VPN access | a client key the operator said came from a May 2024 email leak
fact: Reached | the internal network
A person supplied the material, and the agent did the mechanical work of turning it into a foothold.
notes:
At a Greek government entity, the operator gave the agent a 2022 directory export containing authentication keys that had likely not been rotated since. The agent used them to forge an administrator token, which the page maps to T1606, Forge Web Credentials.

It then used VPN configurations and a VPN client key, which the operator described as coming from a May 2024 email leak, to reach the internal network. The page tags that as T1133, External Remote Services.

The page draws its lesson from where the keys came from. A person supplied the material, and the agent did the mechanical work of turning it into a foothold.

Keep both hedges when you tell this. The keys were likely unrotated, and the leak is the operator's own description.

== At a US pharmaceutical organization, the metadata service yielded a fleet role
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.005 | Unsecured Credentials: Cloud Instance Metadata API
technique: T1651 | Cloud Administration Command
fact: Obtained | a role able to command thousands of instances
fact: Then | escalation to an enterprise administrator account
fact: Control | IMDSv2, plus instance roles scoped to one workload
fact: Cloud provider | not named on the page
A role that can command a fleet turns one compromised host into all of them.
notes:
The page calls this the most directly cloud-focused step in the report.

At a US pharmaceutical organization, the agent queried the cloud Instance Metadata Service and obtained a role capable of issuing administration commands to thousands of instances. It then escalated to an enterprise administrator account. The page maps the metadata query to T1552.005 and tags T1651, Cloud Administration Command.

Cloud security teams have known this path for years. A workload's instance role is reachable by anything running on the workload, and a role that can command a fleet turns one compromised host into all of them.

The page names the control as IMDSv2, plus instance roles scoped to one workload. It does not name the cloud provider involved, and the slide says so. The next slide explains what IMDSv2 changes, in general terms.

== What IMDSv2 changes
layout: background
source: AWS EC2 documentation | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/configuring-instance-metadata-service.html
credit: Also from Retrieve security credentials from instance metadata, and Configure the Instance Metadata Service options (docs.aws.amazon.com)
Code on an EC2 instance reads the instance role's temporary credentials from the Instance Metadata Service. IMDSv1 answers a plain request. IMDSv2 needs a session token from a PUT request, and an instance can be set to accept only IMDSv2.
notes:
Background, from AWS's EC2 documentation, in general terms.

The Instance Metadata Service runs on every EC2 instance. Code on the instance reads metadata from it, including the temporary security credentials of the role attached to the instance, which AWS rotates automatically.

IMDSv1 is a simple request and response. IMDSv2 is session-oriented: a client first sends a PUT request for a session token, then includes the token in each request. AWS rejects a token PUT that carries an X-Forwarded-For header, and by default the PUT response has a hop limit of 1. AWS links these changes to defense in depth against open firewalls, reverse proxies and server-side request forgery.

An instance, or an account's defaults, can require IMDSv2, so that IMDSv1 calls fail. AWS warns that enforcing it breaks applications and agents that still use IMDSv1, so test before you switch.

Also from Retrieve security credentials from instance metadata: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/instance-metadata-security-credentials.html

And from Configure the Instance Metadata Service options: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/configuring-instance-metadata-options.html

== Unauthenticated orchestration gave root; LAPS and delegation abuse worked on the domain
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Execution and Privilege Escalation
technique: T1609 | Container Administration Command
technique: T1078.002 | Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts
fact: Containers | a second pharmaceutical intrusion, root in production
fact: Windows side | reported actions include reading LAPS passwords
fact: Also reported | resource-based constrained delegation, to impersonate a privileged account
Neither technique is rare. Seeing both in one campaign shows how many kinds of environment this operation covered.
notes:
A second pharmaceutical intrusion abused an unauthenticated orchestration service to gain root access in a production container environment. The page maps that to T1609, Container Administration Command.

On the Windows side, reported actions include reading LAPS passwords and using resource-based constrained delegation to impersonate a privileged account. The page tags T1078.002, Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts. Note the words reported actions include: the page gives examples, and does not claim a full record.

Neither technique is rare. Seeing both in one campaign shows how many kinds of environment this operation covered with agents doing the work.

The next slide explains LAPS and resource-based constrained delegation in general terms.

== What LAPS and resource-based constrained delegation do
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Windows LAPS overview | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-overview
credit: Also from Kerberos constrained delegation overview (learn.microsoft.com)
Windows LAPS rotates each device's local administrator password and stores it in Active Directory or Entra ID, where authorized administrators can read it.

Resource-based constrained delegation lets a service's owner list which accounts may impersonate users to it.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Windows LAPS automatically manages and backs up the password of a local administrator account on Active Directory-joined or Microsoft Entra-joined devices. Microsoft explains why: local administrator accounts often share one password across many devices, which bad actors can exploit to move laterally. LAPS rotates each device's password, stores it, and lets authorized administrators retrieve it. Access control lists and optional encryption protect the passwords it stores in Active Directory.

Kerberos constrained delegation restricts the services to which a server can act on a user's behalf. In the resource-based form, the administrator who owns the resource decides which front-end accounts may impersonate users to it, through the PrincipalsAllowedToDelegateToAccount setting.

Microsoft also describes the risk delegation carries. If a server hosting a front-end service that delegates to resource services is compromised, those resource services could also be compromised.

Also from Kerberos constrained delegation overview: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/kerberos/kerberos-constrained-delegation-overview

== The operator told agents to find hosts without a sensor
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Discovery
technique: T1518.001 | Software Discovery: Security Software Discovery
technique: T1090.003 | Proxy: Multi-hop Proxy
fact: Where | a global pharmaceutical organization
fact: Instruction | find Windows domain hosts with no Falcon sensor
fact: Purpose | concentrate follow-on activity on unmonitored machines
fact: Network | Tor, SOCKS proxies, a compromised relay, Chisel and SoftEther
Coverage gaps became a target list.
notes:
At a global pharmaceutical organization, the operator instructed the agents to find Windows machines in the domain with no Falcon sensor, judging by process lists and folder contents, so that follow-on activity could concentrate on unprotected hosts. The page maps this to T1518.001, Security Software Discovery, under the page's Discovery phase.

Coverage gaps became a target list.

Traffic ran through Tor, SOCKS proxies and a compromised relay, with tunneling over Chisel and SoftEther. The page tags that as T1090.003, Multi-hop Proxy.

The question for any defender is whether they know which of their own hosts have no sensor, because the unmonitored machines are the ones this operator went looking for.

== BLOODALCHEMY, side-loaded at two ministries
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Stealth and Collection
technique: T1574.001 | Hijack Execution Flow: DLL
technique: T1114.002 | Email Collection: Remote Email Collection
fact: Where | Middle East interior and Latin American foreign ministries
fact: Implant | BLOODALCHEMY, first publicly documented in October 2023
fact: Loader | legitimate GameHook binary renamed svchost.exe, malicious DLL
fact: Collection | mailboxes read on a compromised mail server
CrowdStrike says this actor has mainly pursued long-term access; the agents sped up the path, and the goal stayed the same.
notes:
CrowdStrike reports the BLOODALCHEMY backdoor, first publicly documented in October 2023, at a Middle East interior ministry and a Latin American foreign ministry. The adversary renamed a legitimate GameHook binary to svchost.exe and used it to load a malicious DLL, which decrypted the payload. The page maps that to T1574.001, Hijack Execution Flow: DLL.

Mailboxes on a compromised mail server were also read, which the page tags as T1114.002. That fits an actor CrowdStrike says has mainly pursued long-term access to government, think tank, energy and extractive organizations since 2021. The agents sped up the path to that goal, and the goal itself stayed the same.

CyberScoop reported the campaign as active on October 30. As with stage 01, the page ties no defense to this stage.

== What DLL side-loading is
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1574.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/001/
Side-loading puts a malicious DLL beside a legitimate program, so the program loads it when it runs. ATT&CK says adversaries likely use it to mask actions under a legitimate, trusted process. Benign executables used this way may not be flagged.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

A DLL is a library of code and data that several programs can use at once. Side-loading hijacks which DLL a program loads: the adversary plants a malicious DLL alongside a legitimate application and runs that application, which loads the payload.

ATT&CK says adversaries likely use side-loading to mask their actions under a legitimate, trusted and potentially elevated process, and that the benign executables used this way may not be flagged during delivery or execution. Payloads may also stay encrypted, packed or obfuscated until they are loaded into the trusted process's memory.

ATT&CK lists related forms of DLL abuse as well, including search order hijacking and substituting a malicious DLL for a valid one.

== Commands an hour, drawn to scale
layout: bars
bar: 40 | Typical hands-on activity, up to about 40
bar: 1,500+ | What ARTEX could exceed
Adam Meyers of CrowdStrike gave both figures. CyberScoop reports requests as often as every 10 seconds, with several attack paths running at once.
notes:
This comparison is the one the page keeps coming back to.

Adam Meyers of CrowdStrike puts typical hands-on activity at up to about 40 commands an hour, and said ARTEX could exceed 1,500. The bars share one scale from zero, so the short bar is drawn true to size beside the long one.

CyberScoop also reports requests as often as every 10 seconds, with multiple attack paths running concurrently.

Attribute both figures to Meyers when you use them. The 1,500 is what Meyers said ARTEX could exceed, and the 40 is his description of typical hands-on work.

== Old techniques at a new rate
layout: text
CrowdStrike's Adam Meyers told CyberScoop the attacks showed nothing new in technique. What changed is the rate.
- Agents ran several paths at once and switched when one failed.
- CrowdStrike says agents' breadth likely lets a single operator reach more targets.
notes:
Meyers told CyberScoop the attacks showed nothing new in technique. The stages bear that out where the page says so: cloud security teams have known the metadata path for years, and of stage 05 the page says neither technique is rare.

What changed is the rate. Agents pursued several attack paths at once and switched to another when one failed. Recovered transcripts show instructions labeled as human input directing agents to tasks from writing web shells to inventorying endpoint sensor coverage, and CrowdStrike says this breadth likely enables a single operator to apply more techniques against more targets at once. As before, keep the word likely, because the page does not say how many operators ran the campaign.

The page also treats tempo as a signal worth hunting. It comes up again after the defenses.

== Seven defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: SAML validation, appliance patching | 02
row: Key rotation | 03
row: IMDSv2 and single-workload instance roles | 04
row: Orchestration services locked down | 05
row: Sensor gaps closed, measured by host | 06
row: Hunting markers and tempo | -
row: Decoys and false wins | -
No defense on the page is tied to stage 01 or stage 07.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's seven defenses against the seven stages. A cell is marked only where the page ties that defense to that stage.

SAML signature validation and patching internet-facing appliances answer stage 02. Key rotation answers stage 03, where keys likely not rotated since a 2022 export let the agent forge a token. IMDSv2 and scoped instance roles answer stage 04. Locking down orchestration services answers stage 05. Closing sensor gaps answers stage 06.

Two defenses name no stage: hunting for ARTEX markers and machine tempo, and giving agents decoys and false wins.

Now look at the empty columns. No defense on the page is tied to stage 01, the human-steered agent setup, or stage 07, the backdoor and the mail. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end.

== Rotate old keys, and scope instance roles
layout: compare
col: Rotate keys (stage 03) | Keep an inventory of long-lived signing and export keys | Rotate on suspected exposure as well as on a calendar | Removing an exposed secret from where it leaked does not invalidate it, per Microsoft
col: IMDSv2 and scoped roles (stage 04) | CrowdStrike recommends IMDSv2 directly | Scope each instance role to one workload | Use a separate, tightly held fleet identity, reachable only from a management path
Agents make old, half-forgotten secrets cheap to use.
notes:
Two defenses come straight from stages 03 and 04.

First, keys. The Greek government step ran on keys likely not rotated since a 2022 export, and a VPN client key the operator tied to a 2024 leak finished the job. Agents make old, half-forgotten secrets cheap to use. Keep an inventory of long-lived signing and export keys, and rotate them when exposure is suspected as well as on a calendar, on a schedule you can prove. Microsoft's guidance from a separate campaign it links to ARTEX applies here too: removing an exposed secret from where it leaked does not invalidate it.

Second, the metadata service. CrowdStrike recommends IMDSv2 directly. The page calls the larger problem in this case a role able to command thousands of instances sitting on a single host. Fleet administration should go through a separate, tightly held identity, reachable only from a management path.

== Validate SAML, lock down orchestration, close sensor gaps
layout: text
- Reject unsigned SAML assertions, test service providers, and patch internet-facing appliances.
- Require authentication on every container management API, and keep those endpoints off shared networks.
- Run endpoint detection on servers not reachable from the internet, and measure coverage by host.
notes:
Three more defenses each answer one stage.

For stage 02, enforce SAML signature validation everywhere. An unsigned assertion should never be accepted. Test your service providers for it, and patch internet-facing appliances, since those are where the first request lands.

For stage 05, lock down orchestration services. An unauthenticated orchestration endpoint gave root in production, so require authentication on every container management API, keep those endpoints off shared networks, and scan for exposed ones regularly.

For stage 06, close the sensor gaps an attacker can enumerate. The operator's own instruction shows what coverage gaps are worth to an attacker. Run endpoint detection on servers that are not reachable from the internet, and measure coverage by host, because the unmonitored machines are the ones this operator went looking for.

== Give agents decoys and false wins to chase
layout: compare
col: Microsoft says | Honeypots, decoys, tarpits and fake wins can pull autonomous attacks from real targets | Convincing evidence of a completed objective can make agents stop on a false success | In its own testing, these controls helped reduce attack traffic reaching real assets
col: Check Point found | In one cybercrime operation, ARTEX agents reported successful exploitation | They went by API responses indicating success | The operator's wallet stayed empty
Microsoft's result comes from one vendor's tests.
notes:
This defense names no stage.

Microsoft says honeypots, decoys, tarpits and fake wins can pull autonomous attacks away from real targets, and that convincing evidence of a completed objective can make an agent stop on a false success. In Microsoft's own testing these controls helped reduce the attack traffic reaching real assets.

The page is careful here, and you should be too: that result comes from one vendor's tests.

Check Point's findings point the same way. In one cybercrime operation it studied, ARTEX agents reported successful exploitation on the strength of API responses indicating success, while the operator's wallet stayed empty.

== Hunt for ARTEX markers, and for machine tempo
layout: compare
col: Markers, which change quickly | Command lines containing artx or artex, per CrowdStrike | Cloud roles with an artx prefix, and C:\ProgramData\artx5v\ | ARTEX's default tools, like curl and Nmap, may send default user agents, per Microsoft
col: Tempo, a more durable signal | Command rates far above the 40 an hour CrowdStrike gives for hands-on work | A wide variety of requests in a brief time, per Microsoft | Variations on one technique retried at machine speed
Microsoft warns that blocking indicators like these will not stop an agent.
notes:
The page gives two kinds of hunting signal.

The first is ARTEX's own markers. CrowdStrike suggests hunting command lines containing artx or artex strings, artx-prefixed cloud roles, and the C:\ProgramData\artx5v\ directory. Microsoft notes that ARTEX by default runs tools such as Nmap, curl, wget, Python HTTP clients and headless Chromium through Playwright, whose traffic may carry default user agent strings that can alert you to the activity. Indicators like these change quickly, and Microsoft warns that blocking them will not stop an agent.

The second signal is tempo, which the page calls more durable. Command rates far above the 40 an hour CrowdStrike gives for hands-on work are measurable in any environment. Microsoft describes agentic scanning as a wide variety of requests in a brief time, with variations on one technique retried at machine speed.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can you list every long-lived signing and export key, and when each was rotated?
check: Do your instances require IMDSv2, with each instance role scoped to one workload?
check: Does any single host hold a role that can command your whole fleet?
check: Do your service providers reject unsigned SAML assertions?
check: Does every container management API require authentication?
check: Do you measure sensor coverage by host, including servers the internet cannot reach?
check: Would you notice command rates far above 40 an hour?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether you could list every long-lived signing and export key and say when it was last rotated. Ask whether your instances require IMDSv2, whether each instance role is scoped to one workload, and whether any single host holds a role that can command the fleet.

Ask whether your service providers reject unsigned SAML assertions, and whether internet-facing appliances are patched. Ask whether every container management API requires authentication and sits off shared networks.

Ask whether you can show endpoint coverage host by host, including servers that are not reachable from the internet. Finally, ask whether anything would alert on command rates far above the 40 an hour CrowdStrike gives for hands-on work.

== Which of your detections assume a human's pace?
layout: question
ARTEX could exceed 1,500 commands an hour, per CrowdStrike, against up to about 40 for hands-on work.

Name one alert, threshold or review step in your environment that would behave differently at that rate.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The figures come from Adam Meyers of CrowdStrike: up to about 40 commands an hour for typical hands-on activity, and more than 1,500 possible with ARTEX. CyberScoop also reports requests as often as every 10 seconds.

Ask each person to name one specific example from their own environment before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says about tempo as a signal.

== What the page says about tempo
layout: answer
The page calls tempo a more durable signal than ARTEX's markers, which change quickly.
- Command rates far above 40 an hour are measurable in any environment.
- Microsoft describes agentic scanning as many varied requests in a brief time.
notes:
The page's answer is to treat tempo as the more durable signal.

Indicators like ARTEX's command-line strings and default user agents change quickly, and Microsoft warns that blocking them will not stop an agent. Command rates far above the 40 an hour CrowdStrike gives for hands-on work are measurable in any environment.

Microsoft describes agentic scanning as a wide variety of requests in a brief time, with variations on one technique retried at machine speed.

Bring the group's examples back to this. If an alert or review step assumes a human's pace, the page's guidance is to look at rate as well as at markers.

== What would you add for stages 01 and 07?
layout: question
The page's defenses are tied to stages 02 to 06. None is tied to the human-steered agent setup, or to BLOODALCHEMY's side-loading and the mailbox reads.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 02, 03, 04, 05 and 06, and two more that name no stage. The page ties nothing to stage 01, the agent setup a human steered at decision points, or to stage 07, where BLOODALCHEMY was side-loaded at two ministries and mailboxes were read.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stages 01 and 07
layout: answer
The page ties no defense to these stages; hunting and decoys name no stage at all. Any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against reported details, like the renamed GameHook binary or the mailbox reads.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 01 or stage 07, and this deck adds none of its own. Hunting and decoys name no stage, so the group may argue they reach these stages; that argument is the group's own.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports.

For stage 01, those facts are the agent structure, the models CrowdStrike names, the 1.4 billion input tokens for one intrusion's planning agents, and the Chinese-language instructions labeled as human input.

For stage 07, they are the legitimate GameHook binary renamed svchost.exe, the malicious DLL that decrypted the payload, and the mailboxes read on a compromised mail server.

